# Prompt execution discipline

All implementation work in this repo comes from prompts in the
[Cursor / Claude Code Prompt Library](mdc:docs/ways-of-work/plan/content-pipeline-v2/cursor-claude-prompt-library.md).
The prompts are sequenced; later prompts depend on decisions made in earlier
ones.

## What you must do when running a prompt

1. **Read the entire prompt before writing a single line of code.**
2. **Read the spec reference** in the prompt's "Spec reference" field. The
   spec is the contract; the prompt is the assignment.
3. **Read the "Strategic context (do not deviate)" section.** It tells you
   what decisions have already been made in prior prompts and what is coming
   in future prompts. The "DO NOT pre-build" warnings exist because future
   prompts will build those things — building them now creates conflicts.
4. **Check the file allowlist.** Confirm the files you intend to create or
   modify are all in the allowlist. If they are not, stop and ask.
5. **Check the file blocklist.** Confirm none of the files you intend to
   modify are in the blocklist. If they are, stop and ask.
6. **Check the dependency allowlist.** If you need a dependency not on the
   allowlist, stop and ask. Do not install it.
7. **Implement only what the prompt asks for.** No bonus features, no
   "while-I-was-here" refactors, no consolidations, no out-of-scope file
   touches.
8. **Handle every edge case in the prompt's "Edge cases" section.** They are
   listed because they are known failure modes; if you skip one, the next
   prompt may break.
9. **Write the tests specified in the prompt.** Same path, same assertions.
   Add additional tests if you discover new edge cases — never remove the
   ones the prompt requires.
10. **Run the Definition of Done checklist** before opening the PR. Every item
    must pass.

## What you must not do

- **Do not interpret ambiguity.** If something in the prompt is unclear, stop
  and add a comment in the PR description asking for clarification. Do not
  guess.
- **Do not "improve" the spec.** If the spec is wrong, flag it — do not
  silently work around it.
- **Do not combine prompts.** One prompt = one PR. If you find yourself
  needing things from a future prompt, you are doing too much.
- **Do not refactor outside the allowlist** even if you spot something
  improvable. File a separate issue if it matters.
- **Do not auto-format files outside the allowlist.** Prettier/ESLint on save
  must be scoped to the active files only.
- **Do not skip tests** because "it's obvious." Cursor's track record on
  unstated edge cases is poor — tests are the only protection against drift.

## PR description requirements

Every PR description must include:

- The prompt number and title
- A checked list of every Definition of Done item
- Any `// TODO(hassan):` notes you added (with the reason)
- Confirmation that no files outside the allowlist were touched
- Confirmation that no new top-level dependencies were added
- Confirmation that `pnpm typecheck && pnpm test && pnpm lint && pnpm build`
  all pass locally

The PR template at `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md` formats this for you.
